#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193682} :activity/id #uuid "e57b078d-2a16-5382-988b-203a38260e4a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346 :topic/id #uuid "977d7a77-a9aa-53d4-9b35-0ca8240c298e" :topic/math-academy-id 4124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192390 :question/id #uuid "3ad1cf25-eb36-5946-9e06-16896691aaea" :question/math-academy-id "e-10112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea184595-346e-5c0c-bb95-8779d73f6f6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443720}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d5b9252a-b7f5-52fe-a10d-b66f5fe665fb" :step/math-academy-id 38134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193898 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample \n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0100980e-b08d-530a-9a07-668209e0af44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7002 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "13d18ec0-09d2-5510-8edc-00b0f1781b94" :step/math-academy-id 42269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192519 :question/id #uuid "4d597364-c8aa-5848-843b-bfc870ebe737" :question/math-academy-id "e-11119"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50df0342-4c06-5c50-90ae-812403a69cee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443721}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "012bca18-0bfe-527e-a3ee-dcfee75eafd8" :step/math-academy-id 42275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192520 :question/id #uuid "5df5188f-3dfe-599f-b6f3-16b93c433ab1" :question/math-academy-id "e-11120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0ab99b4-70b8-5638-bbad-763ef925021c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443723}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f297c08f-8ed0-5ea9-a51f-0fdf821d69d8" :step/math-academy-id 42276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193901 :tutorial/content "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :tutorial/id #uuid "7fa77114-72fa-5d0d-89f5-c85ec7793285" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7015 :tutorial/title "Likelihood vs Log-Likelihood"} :step/id #uuid "46ba308c-f318-5b7c-a01b-76e427c7a010" :step/math-academy-id 42317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193506}}] :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192933}]}
